Chapter 3: A Tool for Killing

	Forcing himself from the chair, Jorrin finally decided to leave the chamber. He walked through the shelves, between volumes, maps, and sketches, and entered the narrow passageway. The smell of old paper was replaced with that of fermenting grain as he stepped into the cellar. He stared at the casks for a moment, at the sunlight that spilled from the stairwell. How could he ever go back?
	
Wiping his face with his sleeve, he began feeling along the wall. A crescent moon, yes, that was it. It didn’t take long to find the engraved brick, and when he pushed on it, the passage sealed itself with a low rumble. Stone slid against stone until the wall looked as it always had.
	
Only then did Jorrin remember. He had left his climbing stick and sack upstairs. For a moment he considered just walking upstairs as if nothing had happened, as if he had been in his room, but what if Odo had checked to see if he was there? They probably figured he was outside. 
 
  
He was also unsure if he was ready to face Mary and his uncle just yet.
	
Moving quietly, he crossed the cellar and climbed the stairs leading to his room instead. The brewery sounded strangely alive again. Faint voices drifted from below, a chair scraped against the floor, someone laughed.
	
Closing his bedroom door, Jorrin leaned and fell against it. The room was exactly as he had left it. His ticket outside was also here. Crossing the room to his window, he pushed the shutters open, letting cool mountain air rush inside. From here, the terraces stretched beneath him in uneven bands of shades of green. For a moment, he had forgotten that the world had just come crashing down on him.
	
Swinging a leg over the sill, he pushed himself off from the window, hitting the ground in a roll.
	
For a while, Jorrin just walked. Past irrigation ditches, past stone walls keeping up terraces, past farmers bent over rows of crops. The familiar smell of the earth seemed to be at odds with him. How could he ever go back?
	
Several people greeted him, but he did not answer. His feet carried him across the lower terraces until the brewery had vanished behind the fields. Only then did he realize where he had come.
	
Corn. Its tall stalks swayed in the afternoon wind. Jorrin stopped at the edge of the field. I imagine you’ve grown. 
	
Jorrin felt a strange ache in his chest. He stepped between the rows, the corn rising above his shoulders, leaves brushing against his arms as he wandered deeper. He wasn’t sure, but it all seemed smaller than he had remembered. 
	
Closing his eyes, Jorrin saw a man laughing. Firm hands lifted him into the golden sunlight. For a moment, it almost felt real. Opening his eyes, it slipped away again.

	“Where did you go?” Jorrin whispered.

	A gust of wind whistled through the rows of corn. No answer.

	He stared at the fields for a long while, the corn rustling around him. Beyond the rows, he could hear distant voices from neighboring terraces. Farmers calling to one another, a dog barking at his flock of sheep, a creak from a rolling cart wheel. These sounds had once given him comfort. It was his proof that there was life in the terraces. It was how it always had been.

Everything seemed normal. 
In fact, nothing had actually changed.

	
His gaze drifted toward the slope below. Somewhere beyond the terraces lay the great wall. Beyond the wall lay the fog. And beyond even that, lay an entire world.
	
Shouldn’t he have been excited? Wasn’t he the one who had always believed that Armadon could not have been everything? Why, then, did he feel so… afraid?

	Was this where he belonged?

	The wind shifted, bending the stalks around him in slow waves. It was getting cold. The sun had begun to sink, painting long shadows across the terraces. His uncle was probably wondering where he was.
	
Pulling himself from the fields, he turned in the direction of The Stump. The grass rustled at his boots as he walked, fireflies blinking at him from nearby trees. The wind whistled louder as the sun sank lower, urging him onward.
	
Jorrin had quickened his pace to avoid the cold, but then the brewery came into view. The Stump stood where it always had, its roots sprawling across the terraces like the fingers of an ancient giant, smoke drifting lazily from its vents. 

	Home. This was home…

 But Jorrin slowed. Each step felt heavier than the last. Somewhere beneath that brewery, beneath what was once an ancient tree, sat a chamber filled with maps and letters written in his father’s hand. Odo had known. He had always known. 
	
Jorrin didn’t know how to face him, but he knew he would have to. 
	
Warm light spilled from the windows, voices drifting from inside. One of them belonged to Mary, the other to his uncle. Taking a deep breath, Jorrin pushed open the door.

	“Ho!” Odo exclaimed, turning towards the door. “What took you so long, boy?”

	“I–”

	“I saw you left your backsack, but you were nowhere to be found.” Odo said, setting up plates at one of the tables. “No matter, come on, customers will come flooding in at any second. It’s time to eat.”
	
Jorrin stood in the doorway for a moment. Odo hadn’t asked him where he’d been. He hadn’t remembered to think of an excuse, so perhaps that was a good thing. Mary sat at the table watching him quietly. He thought he saw her smile. Again. 

	“Well?” Odo asked. “Did Lukin rob me blind?”

	“Not this time.” Jorrin faked a laugh. 
	
	“A shame. He’s getting soft in his old age.” Odo says, using a ladle to pour soup onto a bowl.
	
Pulling a chair for himself, Jorrin sat down quietly. Bread and Lentil Soup. The usual. Wait, on closer look, it wasn’t lentil soup, but lamb soup. Right, the “something special” that Odo had mentioned that morning. 
	
Odo and Mary sat down across from him, and the three began eating.

	“So. How is Lukin?” Odo asked.

	“Fine.” 

	“Was there anything new in the district?” Odo asked.

	“Fi– I mean, no. No.” Jorrin replied. 

	“No?” Odo paused with his hand halfway to his mouth. “You usually have a story for me.” He said, sounding genuinely disappointed.

	Jorrin didn’t say anything. No one said anything for a while. The cackling stove filled the silence.

	Finally, Mary cleared her throat. “You seem quiet today.” She said, “Something on your mind?”

            Jorrin looked up. Mary’s expression was calm, but her eyes remained fixed on him. Across the table, Odo suddenly seemed very interested in his soup. 
            Jorrin felt his jaw tighten. There were hundreds of questions he wanted to ask. Why had Odo hidden the chamber? Why had he hidden the letters? What had really happened to his parents? His gaze drifted between the two of them, then he set his spoon down.

            “Actually,” he said quietly, “there is something I wanted to ask.” 

            Odo froze. 

             “Uncle,” he said, pausing. “what really happened to—“

	The front door slammed open, and a drunken laugh echoed through the brewery. Silver sleeves. Polished boots. The cityfolk from that morning. Odo hurriedly rose from his seat.

	“You watch yourselves with my door!” Odo said, shaking his fist at them. 

	“Or what?” One of the men barked a laugh. “You better give us some booze if you know what’s good for you, old man!” The three stumbled, smelling strongly of ale. One slumped heavily against a table, while another dragged a chair across the floor with a long, scraping screech.

	“Uncl–”

	“It looks like to me you’ve had one too many drinks.” Odo said firmly, stepping slightly in front of jorrin, his arm thrown to the side. “I cannot help you.”

	“You can’t help me? Well of course you can. I’m thirsty, and you’re a brewer. Seems pretty damn simple to me!” His companions erupt into laughter.

	The cityfolk moved close, but Odo didn’t step back. Jorrin felt his teeth clench. He moved to rise, but Odo’s hand snapped out again.

	“Alright, old brewer. Let me ask you again,” One of them said, reaching to his side. The sound of metal sliding against leather cut through the room. “Can we have something to drink?”

The blade slid fully free, and then the man pointed at Jorrin’s uncle. 

“What do you say? Eh?” He said.

Something in Jorrin’s chest twisted, and he stood. 

“Sit, Jorrin.” Odo exclaimed quietly.

“Ah,” The man with the blade smirked slightly. “There we go. Get us your finest ale, boy.”

Jorrin took one step forward, then another. Behind him, he thought he heard Odo say his name again. The blade tilted slightly toward him.
For a moment, everything spun around him like a top, all balancing on a small tip. The laughter, the smell of ale, the hidden chamber, the map, the letters, and the lies. 
Why hide it? Why lie? Why keep me here?

The man’s grip tightened on his hilt.

	“Last chance,” he said.

That was when Odo finally moved. And Jorrin understood something simple. If he waited, his uncle might get hurt. If he stayed still, nothing would change. He couldn’t think, but he stepped in.

	Jorrin’s hand shot up, knocking the blade from its line of action, forcing it away from his body as he lunged forward shoulder-first into the man’s chest, the impact driving the air out of him. The man staggered back a step.
	Jorrin followed immediately, grabbing the man’s wrist with both hands, forcing the arm outward and away from the table, the blade scraping across wood instead of flesh. A mug tipped and shattered on the floor. 
	The second cityfolk stood up too fast, knocking his chair back.

	“Hey!”

	Odo shouted something, but Jorrin didn’t hear.

	The first man tried to pull his arm back, but jorrin stepped closer instead. He could send him flying against the wall, but he’d still have his blade. The man tried to wrench free, but Jorrin twisted his wrist downward, his blade dropping and clattering onto the table. 
	
The second man lunged, arms out, trying to shove Jorrin off balance. Jorrin turned too slowly, and the shove hit his shoulder and sent him a step sideways, but he caught himself on a table edge. Behind him, Odo moved in. 

	“Stop this–”

	But one of the cityfolk swung a fist at Odo. He leaned back just enough that it missed his face and clipped his shoulder instead.
	
Jorrin grabbed the arms of the man who had shoved him. The cityfolk tried to pull away, but nothing happened. Confusion flared across his face. It was like trying to drag a tree by its roots. Then Jorrin shoved. The man stumbled backward several paces, crashed through a chair, and hit the floor hard enough to knock the air from his lungs. 

The first man, now unarmed, swung again. Jorrin ducked, the fist passing over his shoulder, and drove his fist into the man's stomach, sending him stumbling backward.
	
The third cityfolk kicked the table, its wooden edge slamming onto Odo’s leg. The old brewer stumbled.

	“Uncle!”

	The first man recovered faster than Jorrin expected. Snarling, he lunged forward and wrapped both his arms around Jorrin’s waist. Jorrin braced himself, his boots digging furrows into the wooden floor. The man strained, face red, trying to force him down. Jorrin barely moved. For a brief moment, they remained frozen. Then Jorrin planted his feet, and the cityfolk’s eyes widened. 

“No, no, no–” but it was too late.	

	Jorrin shoved. The man broke away as if thrown by a charging ox and crashed into another table. The table folded beneath him with a crack of splintering wood.

	One left. 
	
	The third cityfolk stared at the wreckage around him. One companion lay groaning amid the remains of a chair, and the other was struggling to pull himself from a collapsed table. The drunken grin was nowhere to be seen on his face. His eyes darted toward the door, then toward the blade still lying on the ground. Jorrin caught the glance. No.

	“Don’t!” Odo snapped.

	The cityfolk lunged for the sword, but Jorrin’s hand got there first.

	Jorrin’s fingers closed around the hilt. For a moment, everything seemed to slow. The leather grip fit strangely well in his hand. The weight felt familiar. Something flickered in his mind. Steel flashing beneath the sunlight, a voice correcting his stance. 

	“Jorrin!” It was Odo’s voice. The brewery rushed back into a focus. The cityfolk were still in front of him, and he still gripped the sword in his hand. Then, without a word, he turned the blade forward and drove it into the floorboards. The point sank deep into the timber with a loud crack. 
	The man stopped dead. The sword quivered between them.

	“Leave.” Jorrin said. 

His voice sounded calmer than he felt. Nobody moved. Then one of the men scrambled to his feet. Another followed. Within moments, all three had backed toward the door. They scurried out of The Stump, not bothering to close the door.

Silence settled over the brewery. Only the creak of wood and the distant whistle of wind remained. The sword still stood in his grip, the blade still embedded in the floor. He stared at it for a moment before releasing the hilt. His hand felt strangely empty without it. 
Across the room, Odo slowly straightened himself, a chair overturned beside him. Mary remained seated, watching. Neither of them spoke. Jorrin followed their gaze. 
The sword, then him. A knot tightened in his stomach. 

“Jorrin…” Odo said. He sounded almost afraid. 

Jorrin looked up. The old brewer’s eyes were fixed on the sword. The blade still stood between them, buried in the floorboards. 

“Do you know what that is, my boy?” Odo swallowed.

Jorrin frowned. “A sword.”

“No.” Odo said. His gaze never left the blade. “It is a tool for killing.”

“A plow prepares fields. A hammer builds houses. A scythe mows grass.” Odo’s voice grew quieter. “A sword does one thing, Jorrin.”

Jorrin glanced at the weapon. It looked different. Odo finally met his eyes. 

“Where did you learn to hold it?” Odo asked.

Jorrin froze. Steel flashing beneath the sunlight, a voice correcting his stance. “I don’t know,” he answered truthfully. 

The door creaked against its bearings, and the wind whistled through the open doorway.

“Uncle.”

Odo’s jaw tightened.

“Uncle,” Jorrin repeated. “What happened to my father?”
	
             
